mode: "100644"
text: "# The Repurposing Model — Atomize → Adapt\n\nThe method that separates real repurposing from lazy reposting. Internalize this and every\noutput is native; skip it and you produce duplicate spam.\n\n## Why copy-paste fails\n\nPasting the same caption everywhere fails three ways at once:\n\n- **It fits none of them.** A LinkedIn-length post dies on X; a TikTok caption looks lazy on\n  LinkedIn. Each platform has its own format, mechanics, and audience mindset.\n- **It looks like spam.** People who follow you on two platforms see the identical post twice and\n  unfollow one.\n- **It gets suppressed.** Several platforms down-rank obviously cross-posted content (e.g.,\n  recycled Reels with another app's watermark, duplicate text).\n\nNative-everywhere beats same-everywhere, every time.\n\n## The model\n\n**Atomize → Adapt.**\n\n### Atomize — extract the reusable core\nThe **atom** is the smallest reusable unit of value in the source: the point, the insight, the\nstory, the stat, the moment, the quote. It's platform-agnostic — it's *what you're saying*, not\n*how*.\n\n- A single post usually contains **one atom**.\n- A long piece (blog, 20-min video, podcast, newsletter) contains **many atoms** — each strong\n  enough to be its own post. The skill of long-form repurposing is *finding the atoms*, not\n  summarizing the whole.\n\n### Adapt — rebuild the atom natively per target\nTake the atom and build it fresh for each platform: a new hook, the right format and length,\nnative mechanics, the right CTA, the right register. You're not translating the wrapper — you're\nrebuilding it around the same core.\n\n## What stays vs what changes\n\n| Stays constant | Changes per platform |\n|---|---|\n| The **atom** (core idea) | The **hook** (per platform's mechanics) |\n| The **brand voice** | The **format** (Reel / carousel / thread / post) |\n| The **truth** / facts | The **length** and structure |\n| The brand **guardrails** | The **CTA** (matched to platform + goal) |\n| | **Mechanics** — hashtags, links, tags |\n| | **Register** (voice tone-map: formal ↔ loose) |\n\nIf your two versions differ only in hashtags, you changed nothing that matters — go back and\nre-hook, re-format, re-length.\n\n## The directions of repurposing\n\nEach direction has different mechanics:\n\n- **One → many platforms (fan-out):** one atom, re-natived for each platform. The most common.\n- **Long → short (atomize):** a blog/video/podcast → many short posts, one per atom. The biggest\n  multiplier — one \"hero\" asset can feed weeks of posts.\n- **One format → many formats:** a written insight → a carousel (slides), a Reel (script), a\n  thread. Same platform or across.\n- **Cross-medium:** video → text (pull the spoken insights as written atoms) and text → video\n  (hand the atom to `reels-script`). Never just transcribe or read aloud.\n- **Across time (recycle):** evergreen atoms re-posted later, refreshed — see `content-recycling`.\n\n## The hero → atoms mental model\n\nThe efficient operation runs on a few **hero assets** (a deep blog post, a long video, a big\nlaunch) that each get **atomized into many small posts** across platforms and time. Plan the\nheroes; the atoms fill the calendar. That's how `batch-content-plan`'s capacity math works\nwithout burning the user out on net-new ideas.\n\n## The gate\n\nVolume is the goal, but **quality per platform is the gate.** Five native posts beat fifteen\ncopy-pastes. If a version doesn't feel built for its platform, it's not done.\n"
